          "id": "sec-1-5",
          "secNumber": "1.5",
          "heading": "Analysis models and structural idealization",
          "content": "## Lesson 1.4: Analysis models and structural idealization\n\n\n\n### Physical meaning and governing ideas\n\nFrames, slabs, walls and foundations interact through relative stiffness. A member-by-member hand calculation is useful for checking a global model but cannot reveal all redistribution or torsion. Ideal pinned supports, perfectly rigid joints and constant stiffness are approximations that must be justified by construction details and expected cracking.\n\n\n\n### Analysis and design reasoning\n\nEstablish support restraints, load paths, likely cracked regions, bracing systems and realistic stiffness before extracting design actions. Ask whether second-order deformation amplifies moment, whether construction sequence matters, and whether long-term creep alters indeterminate force distribution. A design drawing is not just a record of forces; it is a set of instructions for constructing the assumed force paths.\n\n\n\n### Worked numerical or geometric illustration\n\nA 7 m beam carrying 12 kN/m has simply supported M_max=12\u00d749/8=73.5 kN\u00b7m. If both ends are truly fixed, the elastic equal-end hogging moment magnitude under the same uniform load is 12\u00d749/12=49.0 kN\u00b7m; positive midspan moment is smaller. Neither expression represents a partially fixed real joint without stiffness analysis.\n\n\n\n### Interpretation and failure checks\n\nCompare the model to a plausible deflected shape and the intended detailing. If a support permits rotation or construction joints weaken transfer, moment distributions change.\n\n\n\n**Study task 1.4.** Reconstruct the main diagram using your own sign convention, then identify one missing input, one model assumption that could fail, one relevant limit state and one construction detail that must be verified. Explain how you would obtain evidence for the missing input.\n\n\n\n---"
