          "heading": "Theoretical Framework: Slabs, Stairs and Floor Systems",
          "content": "> **Learning purpose.** Develop an explicit force, geometry, materials and evidence model before any final code compliance calculation. Numerical examples in this unit are educational and not an authorization to construct.\n\n\n\n### Guided investigation: slab actions in two dimensions\n\nThe simplest slab calculation takes a unit-width strip carrying gravity area pressure. For a simple one-way strip, this converts directly to a line load in kN/m. Its moment is then obtained from beam equilibrium. That calculation provides a good learning baseline because the units are transparent and the moment changes predictably with span. However, it does not show the negative moments near continuous support, torsional corner restraint, concentrated column reactions or the plate action of a two-way system.\n\nSketch one rectangular floor bay three ways. In the first sketch, support it primarily along two opposing edges and annotate the one-way span. In the second, add substantial support on all four edges and draw conceptual bending in both orthogonal directions. In the third, remove the perimeter beams and carry the slab directly on columns. The panel now demands concentrated slab-to-column force transfer and possible punching near supports. Without a correct analysis method, dividing total load between x and y directions based on a guessed ratio is not acceptable. In an initial model, show candidate column and middle strips but do not present them as code-prescribed widths until those rules have been checked.\n\nAdd a rectangular opening near an interior column. The opening removes concrete from potential shear-perimeter paths and changes local bending and reinforcement continuity. Sketch how the critical punching perimeter might be truncated, using the governing code\u2019s treatment rather than the unperforated square formula. If the opening interrupts top steel near a column, new bars may be required around it and must develop their forces. A flat slab that is numerically strong at mid-panel can still have a critical slab\u2013column connection.\n\nDeflection deserves a separate investigation. With gross elastic stiffness, a simple one-way strip has displacement proportional to L\u2074/h\u00b3 under a fixed uniform load and material modulus. If span grows by 20%, the fourth-power effect is about a factor of 2.07 even before cracking. If concrete cracks significantly, reduced effective stiffness can amplify this response; sustained loads can add creep deflection. Evaluate the relative timing of partition erection and slab support removal, since damage to finishes may arise from differential deflection after completion.\n\nFinally trace diaphragm actions. An ordinary floor slab can also transmit lateral inertia to structural walls and frames. Large openings can interrupt collectors and chords, and slab-to-wall connectors require detail. Create a clear division between the gravity design, slab-column connections, serviceability, fire resistance and diaphragm force paths. The project should not be called complete because a single slab bending check passes.\n\n\n\n---"
