          "content": "## Lesson 4.1: One-way slabs and supported strips\n\n\n\n### Physical meaning and governing ideas\n\nAn ideal one-way slab delivers most load to a pair of opposing supports. A one-metre-wide strip provides a useful bending and shear model, but continuity, openings, edge conditions and slab aspect ratio can alter action distribution. Serviceability often sets a minimum viable depth long before a nominal bending calculation becomes difficult.\n\n\n\n### Analysis and design reasoning\n\nCompute load per metre width correctly: an area pressure of 6 kN/m\u00b2 on a 1 m strip gives 6 kN/m. Locate positive moment steel near the bottom in the span, negative moment steel near the top above continuous supports, and distribution or temperature reinforcement as applicable. Reinforcing orientation and lap locations must follow the actual moment field.\n\n\n\n### Worked numerical or geometric illustration\n\nFor a 4.0 m simple span with 6 kN/m\u00b2 area load, a 1 m strip sees 6 kN/m, maximum positive moment 12 kN\u00b7m/m and support reaction 12 kN/m. If an uncracked elastic calculation yields very small deflection, test how cracking and creep may multiply that prediction.\n\n\n\n### Interpretation and failure checks\n\nDo not import beam support continuity rules blindly into slab strips that also participate in diaphragm, punching or transfer actions.\n\n\n\n**Study task 4.1.** Reconstruct the main diagram using your own sign convention, then identify one missing input, one model assumption that could fail, one relevant limit state and one construction detail that must be verified. Explain how you would obtain evidence for the missing input.\n\n\n\n---"
