          "heading": "Theoretical Framework: Columns, Slenderness and Structural Walls",
          "content": "> **Learning purpose.** Develop an explicit force, geometry, materials and evidence model before any final code compliance calculation. Numerical examples in this unit are educational and not an authorization to construct.\n\n\n\n### Guided investigation: a column is a system component\n\nAn axially loaded short column is an appealing introductory example because materials appear to share compressive force. But real columns see bending from beam-end moments, lateral drift, construction imperfections and unavoidable eccentricity. Reinforcement and concrete stresses cannot be assigned independently if both materials are assumed to deform compatibly. A P\u2013M interaction analysis traces the combined capacity for a sequence of neutral-axis positions; this geometry explains why high compression and high bending demand interact rather than add linearly.\n\nConstruct a rectangular section diagram with symmetric layers of reinforcing steel. Assume a linear strain diagram and an extreme concrete compression strain appropriate to the illustrative model. For each neutral-axis depth, compute concrete compression and steel forces from their strain-compatible constitutive relationships. Sum P and M about the section centroid. Plot the resulting points and inspect where one layer yields in compression or tension. A nonrectangular section or a biaxial column requires a more advanced section analysis, not merely a reused rectangular curve.\n\nThe resulting nominal locus is not a code-approved capacity surface. Design reduction factors or partial material factors, tied versus spiral confinement, minimum eccentricity, reinforcement limits and tie spacing need separate application. Even these cross-section checks cannot represent member stability. A slender column under axial force will bend and thereby generate additional moment. A braced frame, where translational sway is restrained, differs from an unbraced frame where column stability interacts across the entire story.\n\nTake a two-story frame and mark where a lateral horizontal force enters the diaphragm and travels into columns or walls. If the frame is intended to resist earthquakes, think about intended plastic hinges and the protection of joints and columns from brittle mechanisms. Inadequate confinement near a joint can invalidate assumptions about sustained plastic rotation, and poorly developed beam bars can prevent a frame from redistributing moment. A wall may reduce lateral drift but introduce transfer and foundation overturning demands.\n\nCreate two check records: one for the cross-section with strain compatibility, and another for the member and lateral system with end restraint, effective stiffness, second-order effects, axial stability and seismic requirements as applicable. An independent reviewer should be able to see where nominal resistance ends and the yet-to-be-performed code checks begin. A large P\u2013M number does not certify a column or a building.\n\n\n\n---"
