          "heading": "Short tied columns under axial load",
          "content": "## Lesson 5.1: Short tied columns under axial load\n\n\n\n### Physical meaning and governing ideas\n\nA reinforced concrete column uses concrete compression and longitudinal steel to resist axial force, but imperfection and accidental eccentricity keep pure axial loading an idealization. Transverse ties restrain longitudinal bars and provide confinement and resistance to bar buckling; their spacing and anchorage are not optional finishing details.\n\n\n\n### Analysis and design reasoning\n\nBegin with gross area, steel area and compatible strain response, then consider minimum eccentricities, load combinations and strength reduction requirements. Recognize that a nominal concentric compressive sum is not the permitted design capacity of a tied column. Column behavior is sensitive to restraint and detailing near beam-column joints.\n\n\n\n### Worked numerical or geometric illustration\n\nFor a 400\u00d7450 mm gross section with Ast=2400 mm\u00b2, concrete area excluding steel is 177600 mm\u00b2. A simple materials sum 0.85\u00d732\u00d7177600+420\u00d72400=5.84 MN is only a nominal upper-bound style thought experiment; it is not an allowable column load.\n\n\n\n### Interpretation and failure checks\n\nCheck column dimensions against the bar arrangement and tie bends: a theoretically adequate area may not be constructible with proper clearances.\n\n\n\n**Study task 5.1.** Reconstruct the main diagram using your own sign convention, then identify one missing input, one model assumption that could fail, one relevant limit state and one construction detail that must be verified. Explain how you would obtain evidence for the missing input.\n\n\n\n---"
