          "heading": "Axial force and bending interaction",
          "content": "## Lesson 5.2: Axial force and bending interaction\n\n\n\n### Physical meaning and governing ideas\n\nA column section under combined axial load and bending cannot be designed from independent axial and flexural capacities added together. A P\u2013M interaction locus follows strain compatibility, concrete stress distribution and the stress reached in each reinforcement layer. The point describing demand must fall within a suitably reduced and code-checked capacity domain.\n\n\n\n### Analysis and design reasoning\n\nGenerate section points by varying neutral-axis position and solving steel strain from plane sections. Sum axial forces and moments about a consistent centroid; preserve the sign convention. For biaxial loading, use an appropriate surface or conservative approved interaction method rather than projecting a three-dimensional demand onto one arbitrary axis.\n\n\n\n### Worked numerical or geometric illustration\n\nA column carrying Pu=900 kN with eccentricity e=80 mm has moment due solely to that eccentricity M=Pu e=72 kN\u00b7m. End moments, second-order effects and lateral loading may substantially increase the design moment and cannot be omitted.\n\n\n\n### Interpretation and failure checks\n\nUse the interaction laboratory to find why a section can have substantial nominal compression resistance yet limited moment capacity at particular axial force.\n\n\n\n**Study task 5.2.** Reconstruct the main diagram using your own sign convention, then identify one missing input, one model assumption that could fail, one relevant limit state and one construction detail that must be verified. Explain how you would obtain evidence for the missing input.\n\n\n\n---"
