          "secNumber": "5.4",
          "heading": "Slenderness and second-order effects",
          "content": "## Lesson 5.3: Slenderness and second-order effects\n\n\n\n### Physical meaning and governing ideas\n\nSlender compression members bend under initial eccentricity and lateral drift, causing additional P\u2013\u0394 and P\u2013\u03b4 moments. Slenderness cannot be judged from physical height alone: end restraint, effective stiffness, axial load and braced or unbraced sway conditions matter. Long-term creep further decreases effective stiffness and raises second-order sensitivity.\n\n\n\n### Analysis and design reasoning\n\nIdentify whether a frame can sway, estimate an appropriate effective length or use a second-order analysis, and include cracked stiffness consistent with the chosen analysis method. Verify stability under governing combinations, with geometric and material nonlinearities as required. A first-order axial\u2013moment check may be unconservative when deflection magnifies moment.\n\n\n\n### Worked numerical or geometric illustration\n\nFor an ideal pin-ended elastic strut EI=40 MN\u00b7m\u00b2 and length L=5 m, Euler load Pe=\u03c0\u00b2 EI/L\u00b2\u224815.79 MN. That is an elastic bifurcation reference, not reinforced concrete code axial capacity. Cracking, creep, imperfections and inelasticity can govern far earlier.\n\n\n\n### Interpretation and failure checks\n\nIncreasing reinforcement does not automatically solve a sway problem. Bracing, geometry and system stiffness may provide more effective remedies.\n\n\n\n**Study task 5.3.** Reconstruct the main diagram using your own sign convention, then identify one missing input, one model assumption that could fail, one relevant limit state and one construction detail that must be verified. Explain how you would obtain evidence for the missing input.\n\n\n\n---"
