          "heading": "Shear walls, coupling beams and boundary regions",
          "content": "## Lesson 5.4: Shear walls, coupling beams and boundary regions\n\n\n\n### Physical meaning and governing ideas\n\nWalls provide lateral stiffness and gravity capacity, but their behavior depends on aspect ratio, flexural axial interaction, shear cracking, confinement and boundary details. A coupled-wall system also develops axial action through coupling beams, redistributing overturning demand. Connections to foundations and diaphragms complete the load path.\n\n\n\n### Analysis and design reasoning\n\nSketch shear, axial force, overturning moment and displacement along the wall height. Determine whether flexure, shear, sliding, wall instability or boundary crushing controls. Under seismic demands, detailed confinement and capacity design can be essential even when simple elastic section calculations predict excess strength.\n\n\n\n### Worked numerical or geometric illustration\n\nFor an elastic 9 m cantilever wall subjected to a 140 kN top lateral force, base moment is 1260 kN\u00b7m. If idealized flexural rigidity is EI=110 MN\u00b7m\u00b2, roof deflection from bending alone is F H\u00b3/(3 EI)=309 mm? Check units: 140\u00d7729/(3\u00d7110)=309 mm. Such a large result should prompt review of realistic EI and load levels.\n\n\n\n### Interpretation and failure checks\n\nDo not transfer a beam stirrup design formula to a wall without examining its in-plane shear fields and code wall detailing requirements.\n\n\n\n**Study task 5.4.** Reconstruct the main diagram using your own sign convention, then identify one missing input, one model assumption that could fail, one relevant limit state and one construction detail that must be verified. Explain how you would obtain evidence for the missing input.\n\n\n\n---"
