          "id": "sec-5-7",
          "secNumber": "5.7",
          "heading": "Knowledge check with explanatory answers",
          "content": "## Knowledge check with explanatory answers\n\n\n\n### Question 5.1\n\nWhy is a gross material sum not an allowable column load?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** An axial sum of concrete and steel stress capacities ignores minimum eccentricity, interaction with bending, reduction factors, confinement differences, reinforcement limits and stability. Real columns carry moments and may be slender. A nominal sum is useful for scale, but it must not be described as design capacity or permitted service load.\n\n\n\n### Question 5.2\n\nWhat is the physical meaning of a point on a P\u2013M interaction curve?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** It represents axial force and moment resultants from one assumed compatible section strain distribution and material stress state. The locus changes when the neutral axis moves, steel layers change stress sign or reach yield, and concrete compression changes. A project demand pair must be checked against the correctly reduced, code-defined resistance region with member effects included.\n\n\n\n### Question 5.3\n\nHow do first-order and second-order moments differ?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** First-order moments come from equilibrium of the original geometry. Second-order moments include the additional effects of axial forces acting through displacements, such as P\u2013\u0394 and P\u2013\u03b4. They can amplify bending in slender or sway systems. Effective member stiffness, restraint and long-term behavior influence the magnitude; a cross-section capacity check cannot by itself determine the amplification.\n\n\n\n### Question 5.4\n\nWhat must a shear wall transfer into its foundation?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** The wall may transmit vertical gravity and seismic axial actions, in-plane shear, overturning moment, uplift and concentrated boundary forces. A foundation must resist them with compatible soil behavior, reinforcement and connection details. The wall also needs a diaphragm connection to receive lateral load. A diagram of wall base shear alone is incomplete.\n\n\n\n### Question 5.5\n\nWhy do tied and spiral columns require different treatment?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** Transverse reinforcement arrangement changes confinement, longitudinal-bar stability and postpeak response. A governing code may prescribe different nominal strength limitations, factors and detailing for tied versus spirally reinforced columns. Counting the same total transverse steel area does not ensure equivalent confinement geometry or seismic performance."
