          "id": "sec-6-5",
          "secNumber": "6.5",
          "heading": "Rafts, combined footings and tie beams",
          "content": "## Lesson 6.4: Rafts, combined footings and tie beams\n\n\n\n### Physical meaning and governing ideas\n\nRafts and combined footings redistribute reactions among multiple supports. The soil behaves as a deformable foundation, so uniform pressure under each column is unlikely when loads and stiffness differ. Differential settlement can induce moments that a rigid isolated-footing analysis misses.\n\n\n\n### Analysis and design reasoning\n\nUse compatible soil and structural models: a rigid resultant check for an initial sanity estimate, then a more appropriate raft-on-springs, continuum or advanced geotechnical model where project demands it. Account for construction joints, punching at each column, edge strips and the need to anchor tie beams into the supported foundations.\n\n\n\n### Worked numerical or geometric illustration\n\nTwo columns carrying 800 and 1400 kN at 4 m spacing have resultant 2200 kN acting 1400\u00d74/2200\u22482.545 m from the smaller column, ignoring footing weight. A rectangular combined foundation should place its contact-area centroid consistently with this resultant for a centered-pressure idealization.\n\n\n\n### Interpretation and failure checks\n\nCheck foundation serviceability with site-specific settlement predictions; larger concrete thickness cannot repair an unrealistic soil model.\n\n\n\n**Study task 6.4.** Reconstruct the main diagram using your own sign convention, then identify one missing input, one model assumption that could fail, one relevant limit state and one construction detail that must be verified. Explain how you would obtain evidence for the missing input.\n\n\n\n---"
