          "id": "sec-6-7",
          "secNumber": "6.7",
          "heading": "Knowledge check with explanatory answers",
          "content": "## Knowledge check with explanatory answers\n\n\n\n### Question 6.1\n\nWhat is the distinction between soil bearing demand and concrete footing strength?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** Soil bearing checks examine contact pressures, geotechnical resistance and settlement at relevant load levels. Concrete footing checks examine flexural, one-way shear, punching, development, crack and durability behavior. Passing a soil pressure limit does not prove that the concrete section can transfer the column action, and passing punching does not prove acceptable settlement.\n\n\n\n### Question 6.2\n\nWhy can footing punching demand change with plan width?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** A wider footing decreases average pressure under the same centered load, but the punching free body excludes or includes a soil reaction area defined by the control perimeter. The net force crossing that perimeter and its perimeter length depend on width, column size and effective depth. The change is not captured by pressure alone and must be compared with proper code resistance.\n\n\n\n### Question 6.3\n\nWhen does a constant soil pressure assumption fail?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** Eccentric loads, overturning, unequal soil stiffness, loss of contact and significant raft flexibility can cause nonuniform contact stress. The soil may be nonlinear or layered, and groundwater can influence effective stress. A rigid centered-footing approximation must be replaced or supplemented as site conditions demand.\n\n\n\n### Question 6.4\n\nWhat is the implication of a blocked retaining-wall drain?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** Water pressure may build behind the wall in addition to earth pressure, increasing lateral force, overturning, sliding demand and possibly uplift. The stability and concrete bending checks must include a credible groundwater scenario. A drainage detail requires maintenance and constructability; it is not inherently reliable without evidence.\n\n\n\n### Question 6.5\n\nWhy should two unequal column reactions be combined by moments?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** The resultant load location follows equilibrium and depends on force-weighted positions, not merely the midpoint between supports. A combined footing that ignores this eccentric resultant may develop nonuniform soil pressure even under otherwise uniform conditions. For columns with force P1 at x1 and P2 at x2, xR=(P1x1+P2x2)/(P1+P2)."
