          "id": "sec-7-7",
          "secNumber": "7.7",
          "heading": "Knowledge check with explanatory answers",
          "content": "## Knowledge check with explanatory answers\n\n\n\n### Question 7.1\n\nWhy can a beam be stronger in bending but worse in crack control?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** A layout with large bars may provide ample area for nominal bending strength yet develop wider crack spacing and less favorable surface crack distribution. Steel stress, cover, spacing, bond and restraint are relevant. Crack control should use code-specific service-load criteria; moment strength alone does not determine crack width.\n\n\n\n### Question 7.2\n\nWhat is the role of transformed-section analysis in deflection?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** It allows a cracked concrete section with steel reinforcement to be represented by an equivalent stiffness under elastic assumptions. Concrete tension is neglected; steel area is scaled by the modular ratio. The resulting cracked inertia offers insight into stiffness sensitivity but is not necessarily the effective inertia at every load level or time.\n\n\n\n### Question 7.3\n\nWhy is a uniform-bond development-length calculation incomplete?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** The equation assumes average \u03c4 remains constant along a bar, neglecting splitting, cover, confinement, placement and surface effects. Real strain and bond distributions are nonuniform. Development and splice provisions account for influences not present in the simple circumference-force balance. The illustrative formula can teach equilibrium but cannot specify a construction lap.\n\n\n\n### Question 7.4\n\nHow does a reinforcement fit calculation support structural performance?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** If intended bars cannot fit within cover and stirrup boundaries, the installed arrangement will differ from the design model or concrete consolidation will be compromised. Actual effective depth, bond, durability and confinement may change. Cage fit must be checked with realistic bar diameters, clear spacing, hooks, aggregate passage and tolerances.\n\n\n\n### Question 7.5\n\nWhy can long-term deflection differ from a short-term estimate?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** Creep under sustained stress, shrinkage curvature and evolving cracking change the stiffness and deformation over time. Temporary formwork and the age at loading influence the history. A short-term gross-section deflection cannot capture partition damage or fa\u00e7ade movement that develops after occupancy."
