          "id": "sec-8-7",
          "secNumber": "8.7",
          "heading": "Knowledge check with explanatory answers",
          "content": "## Knowledge check with explanatory answers\n\n\n\n### Question 8.1\n\nWhat is the purpose of a capacity-design hierarchy?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** It promotes stable inelastic mechanisms and protects brittle force paths under major hazards. In frames, this can involve intentional beam yielding and protection of columns or joints. The specifics depend on structural system and code; a single generic moment ratio does not establish the performance target.\n\n\n\n### Question 8.2\n\nWhy is a carbonation front not a deterministic corrosion-failure time?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** The front indicates a modeled chemical penetration depth, not initiation or propagation of every corrosion process. Moisture, oxygen, cracks, chloride contamination, actual cover and concrete chemistry influence corrosion. The square-root-time coefficient is environmental and material dependent. Crossing the nominal cover is not itself a structural failure criterion.\n\n\n\n### Question 8.3\n\nWhy are tested software controls and numerical spot checks separate requirements?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** A slider may visibly move while the underlying formula is wrong; an equation may be correct while a broken event handler prevents updates. Test interaction, plotted numeric values, resets and export, and independently recompute representative cases. Tests should also cover extremes, units, invalid geometry and clear warnings.\n\n\n\n### Question 8.4\n\nWhy is a deterministic sample count not a failure probability?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** An arbitrary sequence of perturbed inputs has no calibrated statistical meaning. True failure probability needs distributions, dependence, model error, design actions, limit states and reliability methods. Reporting \u201c20 of 100 samples below demand\u201d as 20% failure probability would misrepresent both the model and engineering uncertainty.\n\n\n\n### Question 8.5\n\nWhat makes a final structural design document auditable?\n\n\n\n**Discussion answer.** It records the governing jurisdiction and code edition, inputs with provenance, load combinations, structural model, analysis results, section and member checks, reinforcement details and independent reviews. It identifies unresolved assumptions and the status of design approval. The analysis must match the drawings and site implementation, rather than being a collection of isolated output numbers."
